SM(551,{"n":"1 Corinthians 15 Resumed","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/1 Corinthians 15 Resumed.mp3","d":2244.9,"s":[[0.16,"Come and study holy scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.08,"Father Stephen is a former Protestant pastor and Bible teacher in the Reformed Church, but now an Orthodox priest, serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[20.8,"So St. Paul continues, the last enemy that will be destroyed is death."],[25.68,"For he has put all things under his feet."],[28.0,"But when he says all things are put under him, it is evident that he who put all things under him is accepted."],[33.6,"Now when all things are made subject to him, then the Son himself will also be subject to him who put all things under him, that God may be all in all."],[41.92,"And so Saint Paul is here quoting from Psalm 8, and is talking about how Psalm 8 prophetically talked about how he, God, would place all things under the feet of him, the Messiah."],[58.72,"And so St."],[59.52,"Paul is saying, this is been fulfilled and is being fulfilled and will be fulfilled"],[66.52,"That Christ has won this victory."],[68.68,"God is already, Christ is already ruling, God has already placed them under his feet."],[73.16,"And the time will come when they will be, they will be utterly destroyed."],[76.68,"And of course, he's saying, obviously, God the Father is not going to be under Christ's feet, because there is no opposition between God the Father and God the Son."],[87.0,"And so God the God the Son, Christ in his incarnation, has always been obedient to his Father."],[95.16,"And so when all things are made subject to Christ, then God will be all in all."],[100.68,"And so this is setting up St."],[102.84,"Paul's view, and this is important to understand because this is one of the overarching themes in St."],[104.84,"Paul's epistles Sometimes as a tension or just a distinction between the already and the not yet, the fulfilled and the remaining."],[126.18,"That Saint Paul sees Christ himself as having fulfilled everything already in the victory of the gospel."],[133.86,"But he also sees that there will be a finality to this victory that happens at Christ's appearing."],[141.82,"And this is part and parcel of the basic understanding of how prophecies are find their fulfillment in Scripture."],[150.94,"And so this is important for just interpreting the scriptures as a whole."],[155.58,"Oftentimes prophecies are given in this way."],[159.66,"There is some immediate fulfillment and then that immediate fulfillment serves as a guarantee."],[165.34,"There's something that happens right away."],[167.42,"And then that happening right away right now we see it is the guarantee to us that the rest of it, the rest of the prophecy that might seem more distant, more crazy, more unlikely, more hard to understand, that that too is going to come true."],[183.02,"One of the classic examples of this, of cour,se is uh is in Isaiah 7 14, which is where during the uh what was called the Syro Ephraimite War."],[194.34,"And you can go back and listen to the the Bible studies I did on Isaiah if you want to hear me talk about why the Syro Ephraimite War is a bizarre name for it, since the Syrians were not fighting against the Ephraimites, they were on the same side."],[206.5,"But during the Syro Ephraimite War, uh the northern kingdom of Israel and the Syrians were attacking uh Judah, the southern kingdom."],[216.58,"And uh Isaiah makes this prophecy that a young woman will have a son, uh, they'll name him Emmanuel, and before he's old enough to know good and evil, before he's old enough to have any understanding or wisdom, while he's still a young child, God will deliver, will deliver Judah from this far superior united armies of Israel and Syria."],[239.46,"So what you see there is that this thing is going to happen"],[246.7,"This young woman is going to have a child."],[249.02,"Once you see her have the child, you'll know it's only going to be a short amount of time now and we're going to be delivered."],[254.78,"So it's a sign of the fulfillment of that greater prophecy."],[259.02,"What's interesting about how the apostles then read the Old Testament is that they take that entire prophecy as the first stage."],[269.5,"They say that entire prophecy, both the woman having the child and the deliverance of Judah from Israel and Syria, are the sign."],[281.26,"They're the immediate thing."],[282.78,"And they were the sign of something that was going to happen later."],[284.46,"Matthew does with that precise verse, with that precise prophecy"],[285.94,"This is what St."],[290.82,"He says that was the immediate thing that we saw and that we read about in the Hebrew scriptures."],[296.74,"But what was pointing to was the birth of Christ from the Virgin."],[301.3,"And so when we saw Christ born from the Virgin, we knew that this even greater deliverance, a far greater deliverance than the deliverance of Judah from Israel and Syria, especially since Judah fell to the Babylonians not that long after."],[314.74,"This much greater deliverance was going to take place in Christ."],[319.06,"And so the word fulfillment, remember, literally means to fill full, to fill up to overflowing."],[325.78,"And so this is this is how all the apostles read the scriptures. is they take everything in the in the Old Testament as prophecy in this way"],[338.22,"It gets called allegory, but it's not really allegory."],[352.3,"In the sense we usually think about it."],[354.14,"When we think of Isaiah, Jeremiah, Saint Elias, Elijah, we think of these people as prophets classically."],[361.26,"But everyone in the Orthodox Church, we refer to pretty much every saint of the Old Testament and of the Old Covenant as a prophet."],[369.5,"And they are a prophet in this sense."],[372.22,"That the things that we read that they did and said are all signs, they're all immediate fulfillments and markers of something that's going to come later, or specifically someone who is going to come later."],[388.26,"And so that's that's their reading."],[390.9,"They read all of it as an extended an extended prophecy."],[396.42,"It's all prophecy."],[397.94,"And so once we understand this idea, we can see what what Saint Paul's doing here about Christ's resurrection and our resurrection, about the defeat of the powers and then the ultimate defeat of the powers."],[409.32,"That all of these things have an immediate fulfillment and then a later fulfillment."],[414.36,"And once we understand this, a whole lot of passages in the New Testament make a lot more sense."],[419.2,"So you'll hear these debates."],[420.8,"You know, when Christ in St."],[422.88,"Matthew's Gospel and St."],[424.16,"Luke's Gospel talks about the time of the end, talks about the last judgment, he also seems to be saying stuff about the Romans attacking Jerusalem in 70 AD and in 132."],[435.68,"Well, which is it?"],[436.56,"Which is he talking about?"],[437.76,"Well, the answer is is both."],[440.17,"The answer is one of those is an immediate thing that they're going to see unfold before their eyes, and when they see it, they'll know that what he's saying about the time of the end, which was going to be distant for them, uh, they'll know that what he said about that is true, because the immediate thing has come to pass just as he just as he said it would."],[460.57,"And so St."],[461.53,"Paul now with with the Corinthians, he's addressing these members of the community at Corinth, who either because they think the idea of bodily resurrection is silly from their pagan backgrounds, or because they came from a Jewish background that didn't believe in it."],[478.53,"We talked last time about how there were a significant number of members of the Jewish community who simply didn't believe in the bodily resurrection, and still are to this day within rabbinic Judaism."],[489.89,"And so, for whatever reason they don't believe in it, Saint Paul is saying, look, we already saw the first fruits of it."],[497.25,"We already saw the beginning of it."],[499.89,"Everything that I've taught you about Christ's appearing, about his becoming present with us again, if you want to use the word return."],[507.89,"yt Ehingver I've told you about that, about about the last day, all that is true, and we know it is true because we, including St."],[516.17,"Paul himself, saw Christ risen from the dead."],[519.29,"We know, therefore, that all of these other things are going to come to pass."],[523.29,"That this current, present period is not just the state of things."],[527.53,"And we don't just die and escape from it because it's not very good."],[532.33,"Because there are all of these enemies that we have to face and struggle with and contend with in this life."],[538.09,"All of these uh evils in the world that afflict us, all of the suffering that we wrestle with."],[545.37,"That is not the end."],[546.41,"We don't just escape that when we die and get away from it, but that the day is coming when that Christ's appearing, all of that will be at an end."],[555.65,"All of that will be put to an end."],[557.73,"And so the three enemies that Saint Paul has shown us are these hostile powers who we just talked about, these demonic powers who are seeking to destroy humanity."],[569.57,"The second enemy is what they use to ensnare and enslave and destroy humanity, and that's sin."],[576.85,"That's sin. It's important that for Saint Paul, on only a few occasions, they're rare, he does do it, but on only a few occasions does Saint Paul talk about sins in the plural."],[589.41,"al. Sins as sort of transgressions, as individu actions that we do that are sinful"],[596.45,"For the most part, in the vast majority of cases, Saint Paul talks about sin in the singular."],[603.61,"Sin is a power, sin is a malevolent power."],[607.13,"And this is in keeping with the way sin is talked about again, going all the way back to the book of Genesis."],[612.17,"The first time the word sin is used in the book of Genesis is with regard to Cain."],[618.33,"When God comes to Cain before he commits his murder and says, Sin is crouching at your door."],[623.85,"It wants to master you, you must master it."],[626.73,"And the word for crouching there is actually a Hebrew word that's derived from an Akkadian word that was used to refer to a particular type of demon, a Babylonian demon, that they believed crawled out of cracks in the earth that would sort of crawl across the ground, seeking after hunting after people."],[643.45,"So sin is this power, sin is this enemy for St"],[643.81,"And so that's the imagery there is that that is what sin is like."],[650.21,"Paul, through which the demons uh ensnare people, and then of course, that sin makes people subject to death."],[657.41,"And so Saint Paul sees these three enemies as being overcome in the reverse order in which they come to be."],[666.61,"They come into the world and come to afflict humanity."],[670.61,"First, as he mentioned with Adam and his disobedience and the expulsion from paradise, comes death."],[676.29,"Death enters the world."],[677.37,"Human beings become mortal and subject to death."],[680.13,"Then through Cain and his descendants, sin comes into the world, the power of sin."],[686.37,"And the demonic spirits who who are using it along with Cain."],[688.61,"This culminates in the Nephilim"],[694.49,"And sorry to disappoint people, but I'm not going to go into detail on the giants right now."],[698.89,"But that culminates in the Nephilim in the flood to sort of wash, wash away the uh the sin that has corrupted, has corrupted the world."],[709.21,"And then at the Tower of Babel, when humanity is divided and God steps back from humanity."],[716.09,"Humanity tries to draw God down and control him in response."],[720.65,"God steps back away from the world and governs it through his created angelic beings, who then also fall, become the gods of the nations who are demons, as St."],[732.73,"Paul told us here. in 1 Corinthians, who dominate them"],[736.45,"So at Christ's resurrection, at the resurrection, the anastasis, Christ has arisen and he's judged the earth, and he's judged those powers and dethroned them."],[748.53,"So he now holds all authority in heaven and on earth."],[752.13,"That's why Saint Paul has gone out and he's brought the gospel."],[756.21,"He's brought the call, not just the report of Christ's victory, but the call to return to the Most High God who created them out to all the world and all the nations."],[765.33,"The next enemy is sin."],[767.25,"Sin is the enemy who we struggle with during these latter days."],[771.01,"Those demonic powers, while they're not enthroned anymore, are still around and are still using sin to attempt to corrupt and destroy us."],[778.69,"Paul says, the last enemy to be dealt with is death itself"],[779.61,"And then, as St."],[786.65,"Because when we all have been raised bodily, when we all have been freed from the realm of death, then death will be empty and utterly destroyed."],[798.41,"And so these enemies are finally defeated in reverse order."],[804.17,"And so that is why it is sin and repentance that make up uh the fabric of our Christian life."],[812.65,"The struggle against sin and the struggle to repent is because it is the enemy that we are struggling with and dealing with in this age."],[820.09,"Even though St."],[821.77,"Paul will say at the cross. Christ has really defeated that enemy too"],[826.61,"But there is this already and this not yet element in terms of uh that victory as well."],[835.17,"So now a very non controversial uh section."],[838.45,"St. Paul goes on, uh verse 29."],[842.29,"Otherwise, what will they do who are baptized for the dead if the dead do not rise at all?"],[848.85,"Why then are they baptized for the dead?"],[851.49,"So uh needless to say, there's been a lot of uh ink spilled over that verse uh over the course of of uh recent centuries at least."],[863.73,"And uh the Mormons, uh, our Mormon friends, or as they prefer to be called, uh, our Latter-day Saints friends, have a very sort of literal understanding of this."],[875.93,"They have the idea that someone died who had never been baptized in the Latter-day Saints church, that a member of that church can go and be baptized on their behalf, and that baptism sort of works retroactively to baptize that person even though they're already dead."],[896.73,"And uh, this is why, or one of the main reasons that the Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints, as their full name is, does so much genealogical research and has such vast genealogical libraries because uh they consider it a good deed to sort of work your your way back and make sure that all of ancestors end up end up being baptized."],[920.45,"And so there are a lot of modern interpreters of St."],[924.77,"Paul here in 1 Corinthians who basically say that's what St."],[929.57,"Paul's talking about."],[931.57,"That there were some people doing that."],[933.97,"And a lot of those who are sort of believing Christians will then try to sort of marginalize it."],[941.09,"And they'll say, well, well I mean St."],[942.53,"Paul doesn't say that's a good thing to do."],[944.53,"Or St. Paul doesn't say that, you know, we're doing that."],[948.05,"He's just talking about some people doing that."],[950.13,"And he's saying, well, why would they do that if there's no resurrection?"],[952.77,"Well, there's a basic there's a basic problem with that interpretation."],[956.13,"Because if this is some other weird group doing a practice that St."],[960.01,"Paul doesn't approve of, how would he make any kind of point here?"],[966.27,"I mean, if I came to you and I said and and you, for whatever reason, didn't believe that that there was any resurrection of the dead."],[975.95,"You thought that, you know, you were taught you're Thomas Jefferson."],[979.65,"And you think that Christianity is a good moral philosophy and we should follow it uh while we're alive, but then when you die, that's it, forget it."],[987.57,"And I came to you and I said, Well, okay, but then if there's no resurrection, why are Mormons baptized for the dead?"],[994.21,"You'd look at me and say, Well, the Mormons are wrong."],[998.21,"You're not a Mormon?"],[1000.07,"You think they're wrong, so why would you hold them up as an example?"],[1004.29,"So St. Paul, by making this argument, by making this argument, by saying, this practice demonstrates that the resurrection of the dead is true."],[1014.05,"That's the argument he's making."],[1015.41,"He's saying that there must be some validity to that practice."],[1018.69,"Because if St."],[1019.33,"Paul pointed to an invalid practice, whatever the practice is, he wouldn't make the point that he's trying to make."],[1027.25,"So whatever the practice is that St."],[1029.09,"Paul is pointing to, he's he is by nature of the argument itself, by the fact that he holds it up as evidence, that the resurrection of the dead must be a reality means that he's saying there's some validity to it."],[1041.97,"So we need to dissect this a little more and refer back again to to the the original Greek understand exactly what practice is that Saint Paul uh is referring to."],[1052.89,"And so there's another important uh Greek distinction in Saint Paul, and I've referred to this I know in the Bible study before, but it bears reiterating that Saint Paul makes a distinction in how he talks about the dead based on whether he uses the Greek article."],[1072.57,"Uh the article is the word the, basically, in English."],[1076.33,"It's a lot more complicated than that in Greek."],[1078.41,"I'm not gonna go all into it now."],[1080.09,"There are whole books you can buy on the usage of the Greek article."],[1085.77,"Every Greek grammar on the shelf has a chapter about the article, as a chapter about the word the essentially."],[1093.09,"And uh when you're studying Greek, especially the first time through as a new Greek student, you basically skim that chapter real fast and move on."],[1101.17,"Uh, because it is not exciting at all."],[1104.05,"But it has important aspects."],[1106.53,"So the word, just like in English, the word the serves to make something specific."],[1113.01,"In English, we have an indefinite article, a, and a definite article, the."],[1117.97,"So if I say a horse, I'm just talking about any old horse."],[1121.65,"If I say the horse or this horse, I'm talking about a specific horse."],[1127.01,"That's the basic function."],[1128.93,"So St. Paul talks the same way about the dead."],[1132.85,"And this isn't reflected well in English."],[1135.81,"Because in English, whether there's an article or not, we say the dead."],[1136.45,"English. And we do that because it's better"],[1143.77,"Uh if on Pasca we all sang Christ is risen from dead, that wouldn't flow really well in English."],[1150.98,"We have to put the to make it work in English, we have to put the the there, even though there's not actually an article there in Greek."],[1157.86,"And so Saint Paul sometimes puts the article, sometimes does not."],[1166.42,"When he doesn't, when there's no article, it's just Necrone, dead."],[1170.96,"We t we still translate it the dead."],[1173.68,"But when there's no article there, he's just talking about everyone who's dead."],[1178.64,"It's not specific."],[1180.16,"Everyone who's dead."],[1180.88,"dead The, everyone in the grave."],[1181.12,"When St Or just the realm of death or the power of death."],[1188.02,"Paul uses the article, which would be the which would be in his case, the dead or this these dead, to refer to a specific group of dead, he always uses it."],[1200.82,"St. Paul always, when he uses the article, is referring to deceased, departed Christians, departed members of the Christian community."],[1211.06,"These dead. The dead in Christ, for example."],[1214.9,"He's always using it to refer to members of the Christian community who have fallen asleep."],[1220.66,"That's who the dead with the article are for St."],[1223.38,"Paul. I went through all that, as kind of complicated as it is, because he uses the article here."],[1230.54,"So when St."],[1232.06,"Paul says here, those who are baptized for the dead with the article, he's not talking about people who are baptized for just dead people in general."],[1242.7,"He's also, though, not talking about people who are baptized for departed people who were not members of the Christian community."],[1254.54,"So it cannot be, because of the grammar that St."],[1258.06,"Paul uses, and he's careful about this distinction, it cannot be referring to the Mormon practice."],[1265.66,"It cannot be referring to someone who is a Christian, who's a member of the church. who's a member of the Christian community, going and being baptized for someone who died unbaptized"],[1277.3,"The grammar won't support it."],[1279.3,"That can't be what St."],[1280.98,"Paul is talking about."],[1282.58,"So he is talking about people who are baptized for departed Christians."],[1288.9,"So to understand what that would mean, we have to get into what that word for is."],[1295.7,"They're baptized for, and for the recording, I'm doing scare quotes."],[1301.54,"For the dead, for these departed, uh, these departed Christians."],[1307.62,"The word there that's translated as for as I pair, which sometimes is kind of literally in King James E translated. as on behalf of"],[1318.3,"And that preposition in this usage means with some benefit to, meaning I'm doing something that in some way benefits the object of the preposition."],[1331.98,"So these people being baptized are being baptized to do something for or give something to or attribute something to these departed Christians."],[1347.46,"And when you look into the culture of the time, we look into the cultural background of Corinth, the way in which that preposition is used in these kind of statements, what comes out is that the most likely reference referent for this, the one that makes sense, the usage of that, where one group of people is doing something on behalf of this other group of people that makes sense in this context and fits with the culture in Corinth, is related to the understanding of patronage in the Roman world."],[1379.7,"Patronage was a key social system in the Greco-Roman world."],[1385.54,"People had, we today sometimes use that term."],[1388.9,"We'll talk about like art patrons or uh patrons of a store."],[1394.66,"Where, and it was a relationship between two people, where one person who was more established, generally wealthy, or who uh had more influence, social influence, or political influence, would act as a benefactor, uh as a guardian, as a as a protector for another person who was of low lower social status, younger, less wealthy, just starting out, uh, of less uh cultural influence."],[1425.26,"Um these relationships were formed in all different ways in uh in daily life."],[1432.3,"Everything from the way that might immediately spring to mind, like someone is an artist, someone is a poet or something, and they have a wealthy person who sort of pays their way, who admires their work and pays their way, but to this is how apprenticeship basically functioned."],[1446.86,"When you were going to be a craftsman, if you were a young person who wanted to become a blacks.mith, you would go and find an established blacksmith with an established blacksmith shop"],[1455.94,"And you would go and he would become sort of your patron and pay your way and teach you and train and tell and lead you into it."],[1462.9,"This was true in the entering into the political life, the academic life of the time, uh, with teachers and their disciples at the at Plato's Academy and in other philosophical schools."],[1474.58,"So this idea of patronage was everywhere."],[1477.78,"And it seems very clear that that that's the usage of the word here."],[1482.5,"That's that's what makes sense both in the Corinthian context and in the first century, and in the context of St."],[1490.66,"the grammar here that St."],[1492.18,"Paul uses. It seems fairly clear that he's talking. about what we would call people who are baptized with a patron saint"],[1501.9,"We are already at this point in the 50s AD."],[1505.82,"So there are already Christians who have fallen asleep."],[1509.18,"There are already Christians who have uh fallen asleep in the Lord, who, as Saint Paul will say, their lives are hidden in Christ, they are with Christ, they are in paradise, whatever language we want to use, they are now spiritually established by virtue of that."],[1527.1,"They have now received in part, there's that already and not yet still, but they have received, they have received their reward to some extent, and so now new people entering the Christian community, not all of them, this practice is just beginning."],[1540.22,"Paul says those who do this."],[1540.3,"not So this is something that some people are doing and others in the mid-50s AD."],[1543.22,"That's why St."],[1550.82,"We're only about 20 years after Christ's resurrection."],[1554.74,"But this is beginning to happen."],[1556.34,"They are coming, they're being baptized into the faith."],[1559.3,"They are taking the name and taking as a patron, one of the saints, one of the one of the departed brethren who has gone before them, who is seen as now being established with Christ in the heavenly places, and therefore able to serve as their patron in life."],[1575.22,"And this practice, of course, has continued all the way down, all the way down to this day of the Orthodox Church."],[1581.62,"This was either for I think all of us who are in the Orthodox Church, this was either done for us as a child when we were when we were baptized or when we were baptized or chrismated and received into the church."],[1593.34,"We chose or were given a patron saint, someone who has gone before us, who who is our patron."],[1600.7,"And so this is this is what Saint Paul is referring to here."],[1604.14,"And so the point he's making is he's pointing to this practice that had already begun and had already begun in Corinth so that he could point to it there and say you know hey if if there's no resurrection from the dead, what are they doing?"],[1617.9,"What would be the point of that?"],[1619.5,"What would be the point of having a heavenly patron if there's no if there's no resurrection from the dead?"],[1625.22,"That makes no sense."],[1626.62,"There's no way to have that relationship that they're establishing through this baptismal practice if that uh departed person is just gone."],[1635.94,"There's no resurrection."],[1637.46,"So that's what St."],[1638.58,"Paul is referring to here."],[1639.86,"He's referring to the beginning of this nascent tradition within Christian baptism that that we've received down to this very day, uh almost 2,000 years later."],[1651.94,"So this is this is uh another, he's giving now another series of arguments."],[1657.8,"So why would we do this?"],[1659.08,"Why why would you do that?"],[1660.2,"Why would anyone do this if if the resurrection weren't a reality?"],[1664.36,"He continues, and why do we stand in jeopardy every hour?"],[1667.88,"So he says, you know, I'm out here risking my life, people."],[1671.62,"I'm out here."],[1672.54,"St. Paul's getting stoned, he's getting shiedpwrecked, he's getting flogg and beaten within an inch of his life."],[1674.98,"Uh he's risking death every day"],[1681.98,"If he doesn't believe that there's anything after, why on earth would he be doing that?"],[1688.38,"Why on earth?"],[1689.82,"Uh why wouldn't he just be, you know, playing it safe?"],[1694.06,"Why wouldn't he be trying to save his own skin?"],[1696.94,"When opposition showed up in a city, why didn't he just get out of Dodge immediately?"],[1701.27,"Before before anything bad happened to him and before his life was in danger."],[1705.35,"He never did that."],[1706.87,"And St. Paul's pointing out, well, look, at least I clearly believe this is true."],[1712.17,"I clearly do it."],[1713.41,"Because we talked before when we're when we're going through St."],[1715.93,"Paul's Epistle to the Romans about how fa faith.ith, the word we translate as faith really is more likefulness, because it includes both"],[1724.85,"Because if you really believe something is true, that's going to shape how you act and what you do and how you behave."],[1730.85,"And so St."],[1731.57,"Paul is using this for an argument."],[1733.33,"He says, Look at how I live, look at how I behave."],[1736.37,"This is an argument that I really believe it's true."],[1739.41,"I'm not just coming to you with this story, oh yeah, I saw Jesus after his resurrection, but clearly I really believe it because I'm out there uh risking my life, uh willing to sacrifice uh my life on this earth over and over again uh in order to proclaim this this gospel uh as true."],[1761.17,"So he goes on."],[1762.05,"I affirm by the boasting in you which I have. in Christ Jesus our Lord, I die daily"],[1767.13,"If in the manner of men I have fought with beasts at Ephesus, what advantage is it to me if the dead do not rise?"],[1774.25,"Let us eat and drink, for tomorrow we die."],[1777.15,"So he says, Listen, if this is it, if you only live once, let's yell YOLO and go party."],[1784.47,"Let's have a good time."],[1785.35,"Why would I be going and getting thrown to the animals in Ephesus?"],[1789.11,"Why would I every day be facing death?"],[1792.39,"Why would I be practicing any kind of asceticism?"],[1797.27,"Why would I do anything in this life looking for a heavenly reward if there is no heavenly reward?"],[1802.07,"Why would I do any of these? things"],[1803.51,"I would just go out and and enjoy this life for as long as it lasts, because once it's over, that's it."],[1807.59,"Paul is doing"],[1810.19,"Once it's over, that's it."],[1811.55,"But clearly that is not what St."],[1815.07,"He says, Do not be deceived."],[1817.23,"Evil company corrupts good habits."],[1819.63,"So that statement in most translations, including the Orthodox uh study Bible here, evil company corrupts good habits, or bad company uh corrupts good morals, I think is the uh king the way the King James uh translates it."],[1833.07,"It's in quotation marks because it's a quotation."],[1835.63,"And you may not know from where."],[1837.31,"It's from a play by Menander."],[1841.87,"Uh Menander uh lived in the generation right after Alexander the Great."],[1849.15,"He lived during the reign of the first. of Alexander's successors"],[1855.67,"So we're talking about around the year 300 BC."],[1860.79,"Menander, as a playwright, sort of won all the awards."],[1864.31,"There were these drama festivals in Greece during his life."],[1868.87,"He won the most prestigious one eight times and won some of the lesser ones as well."],[1874.87,"It's sort of like if you won a bunch of Oscars today, and then a few uh Golden Globes and a few People's Choice Awards that you keep in the bathroom or whatever."],[1884.55,"So this is Menander was sort of the uh theatrical genius of uh the early third century BC."],[1896.31,"And he wrote a play called the The Tais, which was one of his comedies, actually, and that's where this line comes from."],[1897.59,"So St Paul is here actually quoting not the Hebrew scriptures or even anything out of uh the Jewish tradition."],[1911.95,"He's actually quoting to the people at Corinth from uh one of their famous cultural figures."],[1918.11,"So if you ever hear your priest or a priest give a quote from a movie during a sermon, you can't condemn them because St."],[1925.31,"Paul did it first."],[1926.75,"Uh he he used uh this quotation from one of their in a comedy at that."],[1932.83,"I mean, not even a serious drama."],[1935.55,"And so he says, the point he's making with that quote is that, well, look, what does he just been say?"],[1941.79,"He's just been arguing, hey, look, if there's if there's no resurrection and this life is it, then let's just go eat, drink, and be merry."],[1950.87,"Let's just go have fun."],[1952.48,"Let's just live life to the fullest."],[1953.84,"Like Frank Sinatra said, you know, live every day like you're gonna die tomorrow."],[1958.02,"Let's just go do that."],[1959.96,"And now what St."],[1960.92,"Paul is saying is, look, hey, even one of your own pagan dramatists."],[1967.37,"Uh, even one of your own pagan dramatists, uh, said said to you that look, you keep bad company."],[1975.07,"You you you hold fellowship uh with with these kind of people, that's gonna corrupt your good morals."],[1982.19,"So he's pointing to exactly what he's been pointing to all. through the epistle up to this point"],[1987.96,"If you go and you live that way, if you go and you live that way, that way that you used to live."],[1993.18,"That way that you used to live, you're gonna end up in the same pattern of slavery to sin, slavery to those demonic powers, that same cycle of self-destruction that you were in before that Christ freed you from."],[2004.56,"That Christ freed you from."],[2006.08,"So notice what St."],[2007.12,"Paul is doing here."],[2008.24,"He's drawing this direct line between what we would call the theological or the doctrinal or between the content of the gospel, the fact of Christ's resurrection from the dead, and how it proves our own resurrection from the dead."],[2022.0,"He's drawing a direct line from that to the practical effect of how we live our lives."],[2028.92,"And the freedom or slavery, the destruction or life, the sin or the blessing that we experience in this life is directly related."],[2038.6,"Directly related. So the freedom they're experiencing, the growth in Christ they're experiencing, the life they're, the new life they're experiencing now in the Christian community in Corinth, is only possible, is only real, is only a thing because Christ rose from the dead."],[2057.16,"And if Christ rose from the dead, we'll rise from the dead."],[2060.44,"And if that's true, then we can't live that way anymore."],[2064.28,"These things are woven together."],[2066.04,"They can't be separated from each other."],[2068.04,"You can't live like a pagan and worship Christ."],[2071.48,"That's been his point and his main theme all through Fir Corstinthians."],[2074.2,"You're part of the body of Christ"],[2075.2,"Now you're you're a Christian."],[2077.12,"Now you're in the community that belongs to Christ."],[2081.12,"You can no longer live like that."],[2083.04,"You can no longer live like that."],[2085.6,"Because uh you've come out of that, and uh you've come into this community, and so now you need to live this new way, and it's all a product of what Christ has done."],[2095.6,"And so the fact that you have that life, the fact that you have this spiritual experience now, is the proof that Christ is risen from the dead, and therefore the proof that one day you will rise and will give an account for what you've done in this life."],[2110.48,"So St. Paul concludes this section: awake to righteousness and do not sin, for some do not have the knowledge of God."],[2118.16,"I speak this to your shame."],[2120.2,"So he says, Wake up, people."],[2122.12,"Wake up to righteousness."],[2123.4,"It's the same word as justice."],[2124.76,"Wake up to justice."],[2126.04,"Wake up to what is right."],[2128.28,"Wake up to the way the world is."],[2134.2,"And so you need to set things in order now."],[2138.2,"You need to set things in order now."],[2140.44,"Because the person who truly does not believe in the resurrection is the person who lives like there's no resurrection."],[2146.92,"And that person is a person who has no knowledge of God."],[2151.32,"Regardless of what knowledge they claim to have."],[2153.88,"We've seen through 1 Corinthians, St."],[2156.2,"Paul has over and over again been condemning some of the people in their midst for thinking that they have a lot of knowledge and thinking that they have a lot of wisdom, that they do not really possess, and being puffed up with pride."],[2167.6,"And so St."],[2168.24,"Paul is saying, these are the signs that the knowledge of God is not really there."],[2172.8,"And that's why he says, I say this to your shame."],[2175.28,"You should not be proud of this."],[2176.64,"He's just said that he boasts about them."],[2178.96,"He boasts about the freedom they have in Christ."],[2181.36,"He boasts about the salvation that they're experiencing as former pagans."],[2185.6,"But this he is ashamed of that there are some of them who are proving by what they teach and what they do and how they live that they don't have the knowledge of God."],[2194.8,"And that that is the shameful part."],[2197.2,"So we didn't get through a very big section, but as I mentioned last time, 1 Corinthians 15, as sort of this climax of the whole epistle, is very dense."],[2206.32,"It's very dense theologically, very dense in ideas, as the climax of the epistle."],[2212.12,"So this is this is probably a good place to end for this evening."],[2215.88,"So I'm going to end the recording, and once I've stopped the recording, I'll take a few questions from the live."],[2222.6,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[2229.8,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[2235.16,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":20.8,"lv":2,"h":"1 Cor 15:26-28 - Last enemy, God all in all","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>V. 26</b>: \"the last enemy that will be destroyed is <b>death</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Vv. 27-28</b>: \"he has put all things under his feet\"; when all subject to him, the Son himself subject to the Father, \"that <b>God may be all in all</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul quoting <b>Psalm 8</b></li><li class=\"d1\">prophecy that God would place all things under the feet of the <b>Messiah</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>fulfilled, being fulfilled, will be fulfilled</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Christ already ruling, God has already placed them under his feet</li><li class=\"d1\">time will come when they are <b>utterly destroyed</b></li><li class=\"d0\">God the Father obviously not under Christ's feet - <b>no opposition</b> between Father and Son</li><li class=\"d1\">Christ in his incarnation <b>always obedient</b> to his Father</li><li class=\"d1\">when all subject to Christ → <b>God all in all</b></li></ul>"},{"t":100.68,"lv":3,"h":"Already and not yet","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Overarching theme of Paul's epistles</li><li class=\"d0\">Sometimes seen as tension or distinction: <b>already / not yet</b>, fulfilled / remaining</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul sees Christ as having <b>fulfilled everything already</b> in the victory of the gospel</li><li class=\"d1\">but a <b>finality</b> to the victory comes at Christ's appearing</li><li class=\"d0\">Basic understanding of how <b>prophecies are fulfilled</b> in Scripture</li><li class=\"d1\">important for interpreting Scripture as a whole</li></ul>"},{"t":155.58,"lv":2,"h":"Prophecy: immediate fulfillment as guarantee","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Prophecies often given in this way</li><li class=\"d1\">an <b>immediate fulfillment</b>, something that happens right away</li><li class=\"d1\">serves as a <b>guarantee</b> that the rest - the more distant, \"crazy, unlikely, hard to understand\" part - <b>will come true too</b></li></ul>"},{"t":183.02,"lv":2,"h":"Isaiah 7:14","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Classic example: <b>Isaiah 7:14</b>, the <b>Syro-Ephraimite War</b></li><li class=\"d1\">see his <b>Isaiah studies</b> - bizarre name, Syrians were on the same side as Ephraim</li><li class=\"d1\">northern kingdom <b>Israel and Syria attack Judah</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Isaiah: a young woman will have a son, name him <b>Emmanuel</b></li><li class=\"d1\">before he knows good and evil, God will <b>deliver Judah</b> from the far superior united armies</li><li class=\"d0\">What you see: this thing is going to happen</li><li class=\"d1\">she has the child → only a short time now till deliverance</li><li class=\"d1\">a <b>sign</b> of the fulfillment of the greater prophecy</li></ul>"},{"t":259.02,"lv":3,"h":"How the apostles read the Old Testament","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Apostles take the <b>entire prophecy as the first stage</b></li><li class=\"d1\">woman having the child <b>and</b> deliverance from Israel and Syria = <b>the sign</b>, the immediate thing</li><li class=\"d1\">sign of something later</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Matthew</b> does this with that precise verse</li><li class=\"d1\">immediate thing in Hebrew Scriptures pointed to the <b>birth of Christ from the Virgin</b></li><li class=\"d1\">seeing that → a <b>far greater deliverance</b> to come in Christ - Judah fell to Babylon not long after</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Fulfillment</b> literally = to <b>fill full</b>, to overflowing</li><li class=\"d0\">All the apostles read the Old Testament as <b>prophecy</b> this way</li><li class=\"d1\">called allegory, but <b>not really allegory</b> as we usually think of it</li><li class=\"d0\">Orthodox call nearly <b>every Old Covenant saint a prophet</b>, not just Isaiah, Jeremiah, <b>Elijah</b></li><li class=\"d1\">what they did and said = <b>signs, immediate fulfillments</b> pointing to someone who is to come</li><li class=\"d1\">\"They read all of it as an extended prophecy\"</li></ul>"},{"t":409.32,"lv":2,"h":"Applying it: Olivet discourse","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Same idea for Christ's resurrection / ours, defeat of the powers / ultimate defeat</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>immediate fulfillment, then later fulfillment</b></li><li class=\"d1\">makes many New Testament passages make sense</li><li class=\"d0\">Debates: Christ in <b>Matthew and Luke</b> on the time of the end, last judgment - also seems to speak of the <b>Romans attacking Jerusalem in 70 AD</b> and 132</li><li class=\"d1\">which is it? <b>Both</b></li><li class=\"d1\">one is the immediate thing they'll see unfold</li><li class=\"d1\">seeing it → they know what he said about the distant <b>time of the end</b> is true</li></ul>"},{"t":460.57,"lv":3,"h":"Applied to the Corinthians","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Members of the Corinthian community reject bodily resurrection</li><li class=\"d1\">from <b>pagan backgrounds</b> (it seems silly)</li><li class=\"d1\">or <b>Jewish background</b> that didn't believe in it - significant number still don't, within <b>rabbinic Judaism</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul: \"we already saw the <b>first fruits</b>, the beginning\"</li><li class=\"d1\">everything taught about Christ's appearing, becoming present again, the last day = <b>true</b></li><li class=\"d1\">because we, <b>including Paul himself</b>, saw Christ risen</li><li class=\"d0\">Present period is <b>not just the state of things</b></li><li class=\"d1\">we don't just <b>die and escape</b> because it's not very good</li><li class=\"d1\">enemies we struggle with, evils, suffering - <b>not the end</b></li><li class=\"d1\">at Christ's appearing all of it will be <b>put to an end</b></li></ul>"},{"t":557.73,"lv":2,"h":"The three enemies","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>First</b>: hostile powers - <b>demonic powers</b> seeking to destroy humanity</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Second</b>: what they use to ensnare, enslave, destroy = <b>sin</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Third</b>: <b>death</b> - sin makes people subject to it</li></ul>"},{"t":576.85,"lv":3,"h":"Sin as a power","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul rarely talks about <b>sins</b> plural - transgressions, individual actions</li><li class=\"d1\">vast majority: <b>sin in the singular</b> = a <b>malevolent power</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Goes back to <b>Genesis</b>: first use of \"sin\" is with <b>Cain</b></li><li class=\"d1\">God before the murder: \"Sin is <b>crouching at your door</b>. It wants to master you, you must master it\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Hebrew word for crouching derived from <b>Akkadian</b> word for a type of <b>Babylonian demon</b></li><li class=\"d1\">crawled out of cracks in the earth, across the ground, <b>hunting people</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Sin = enemy through which <b>demons ensnare</b> people → subject to death</li></ul>"},{"t":657.41,"lv":3,"h":"Enemies defeated in reverse order","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Overcome in the <b>reverse order</b> they came in</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>First came death</b>: Adam's disobedience, expulsion from paradise, humans mortal</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Then sin</b>: through <b>Cain</b> and descendants, with demonic spirits using it</li><li class=\"d1\">culminates in the <b>Nephilim</b> and the <b>flood</b> - not going into the giants now</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Then the powers</b>: <b>Tower of Babel</b>, humanity divided</li><li class=\"d1\">humanity tries to draw God down and control him</li><li class=\"d1\">God <b>steps back</b>, governs through created angels who fall and become <b>gods of the nations</b>, demons (as Paul said in 1 Cor)</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Resurrection</b> (<i>anastasis</i>): Christ arisen, <b>judged those powers and dethroned them</b></li><li class=\"d1\">holds <b>all authority</b> in heaven and earth → Paul brings the <b>call to return to the Most High</b> to all nations</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Sin</b>: enemy we struggle with in these <b>latter days</b></li><li class=\"d1\">powers not enthroned but still <b>using sin</b> to corrupt and destroy us</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Death last</b>: when all raised, freed from the realm of death, death is <b>empty and destroyed</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Why <b>sin and repentance</b> are the fabric of Christian life</li><li class=\"d1\">Christ really defeated sin at the cross, but still <b>already / not yet</b></li></ul>"},{"t":835.17,"lv":2,"h":"1 Cor 15:29 - Baptized for the dead","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"A very non-controversial section\" - joke</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>V. 29</b>: \"what will they do who are <b>baptized for the dead</b> if the dead do not rise at all?\"</li><li class=\"d0\">A lot of <b>ink spilled</b> over this verse in recent centuries</li></ul>"},{"t":863.73,"lv":2,"h":"The Mormon reading and its problem","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Latter-day Saints</b>: a member can be <b>baptized on behalf of someone dead</b>, unbaptized, retroactively</li><li class=\"d1\">main reason for their vast <b>genealogical research and libraries</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Many modern interpreters say Paul means that</li><li class=\"d1\">believing Christians then <b>marginalize</b> it: Paul doesn't say it's good, just that some do it</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Problem</b>: Paul would have no argument</li><li class=\"d1\">analogy: a <b>Thomas Jefferson</b>-type who thinks Christianity is moral philosophy, no resurrection; \"why are <b>Mormons</b> baptized for the dead?\" → \"Mormons are wrong\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul holds up the practice as <b>evidence that resurrection is real</b></li><li class=\"d1\">so he implies <b>some validity</b> to it</li><li class=\"d1\">need the Greek to find out what the practice is</li></ul>"},{"t":1041.97,"lv":3,"h":"The Greek article and \"the dead\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul distinguishes how he speaks of the dead by <b>whether he uses the Greek article</b></li><li class=\"d1\">article = \"the\"; far more complicated; whole books on it, a chapter in every grammar - students skim it</li><li class=\"d0\">Like English: <b>a horse</b> vs <b>the horse</b> = specific</li><li class=\"d0\">English says \"the dead\" either way - \"Christ is risen from dead\" doesn't flow at <b>Pascha</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>No article</b>: <i>nekron</i>, <b>everyone</b> who's dead, the realm or power of death</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>With article</b>: always <b>departed Christians</b>, members of the Christian community</li><li class=\"d1\">\"the dead in Christ,\" those who have <b>fallen asleep</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Here he uses the article → <b>baptized for departed Christians</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not the Mormon practice: a Christian baptized for someone who died <b>unbaptized</b> - \"the grammar won't support it\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1288.9,"lv":2,"h":"\"For\" = patronage","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Greek <i>huper</i>, \"for\" (KJV \"on behalf of\")</li><li class=\"d1\">means with <b>some benefit to</b> the object of the preposition</li><li class=\"d1\">baptized to do or give or attribute something to departed Christians</li><li class=\"d0\">Corinthian cultural background: most likely <b>Roman patronage</b></li><li class=\"d1\">key social system: established, wealthy, influential person = <b>benefactor, guardian, protector</b> of someone younger, poorer, lower status</li><li class=\"d1\">like <b>art patrons</b>, but wider</li><li class=\"d1\">how <b>apprenticeship</b> worked: a young <b>blacksmith</b> finds an established smith who pays his way, teaches, trains</li><li class=\"d1\">also politics, academia, teachers and disciples at <b>Plato's Academy</b> and other schools</li><li class=\"d0\">Fits Corinth, the first century, and the grammar</li></ul>"},{"t":1492.18,"lv":3,"h":"Baptized with a patron saint","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul means people <b>baptized with a patron saint</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Already the <b>mid-50s AD</b>, ~20 years after the resurrection</li><li class=\"d1\">Christians have <b>fallen asleep in the Lord</b>, lives \"hidden in Christ,\" in paradise</li><li class=\"d1\">received their reward in part (already / not yet)</li><li class=\"d0\">New entrants taking, as <b>patron</b>, one of the <b>departed brethren</b> established with Christ - practice just beginning</li><li class=\"d0\">Continues in the Orthodox Church today</li><li class=\"d1\">at baptism or chrismation we <b>chose or were given a patron saint</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul's argument: <b>no resurrection → what's the point of a heavenly patron?</b></li><li class=\"d1\">no relationship if the departed person is just <b>gone</b></li><li class=\"d0\">A <b>nascent tradition</b> in Christian baptism received down to us, almost <b>2,000 years later</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1651.94,"lv":2,"h":"1 Cor 15:30-31 - Why stand in jeopardy?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Another argument: \"why do we <b>stand in jeopardy every hour</b>?\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul <b>stoned, shipwrecked, flogged</b> within an inch of his life - risking death daily</li><li class=\"d1\">if nothing after, why not <b>play it safe</b>, \"get out of Dodge\" when opposition showed up?</li><li class=\"d1\">he never did</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Faith</b> (Romans) = more like <b>faithfulness</b>, includes belief and action</li><li class=\"d1\">if you really believe, it shapes how you act</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Look at how I live\" - <b>proof he really believes</b>, not just \"I saw Jesus after the resurrection\"</li><li class=\"d1\">willing to sacrifice his life over and over to proclaim the gospel</li></ul>"},{"t":1761.17,"lv":2,"h":"1 Cor 15:31-32 - Beasts at Ephesus, eat and drink","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>V. 31-32</b>: \"I die daily\"; \"fought with beasts at Ephesus, what advantage...?\"; \"<b>Let us eat and drink, for tomorrow we die</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">If you only live once: \"yell <b>YOLO</b> and go party\"</li><li class=\"d1\">why get thrown to the animals, face death daily?</li><li class=\"d1\">why any <b>asceticism</b>, or anything seeking a heavenly reward if there is none?</li><li class=\"d1\">just enjoy this life; once it's over, <b>that's it</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Clearly <b>not</b> what Paul is doing</li></ul>"},{"t":1815.07,"lv":2,"h":"1 Cor 15:33 - Menander","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>V. 33</b>: \"Do not be deceived. <b>Evil company corrupts good habits</b>\" (KJV: bad communications corrupt good manners)</li><li class=\"d0\">A <b>quotation</b>, hence the quote marks - from a play by <b>Menander</b></li><li class=\"d1\">lived right after <b>Alexander the Great</b>, ~<b>300 BC</b></li><li class=\"d1\">won the most prestigious drama festival <b>eight times</b>, plus lesser ones</li><li class=\"d1\">like winning Oscars, then \"Golden Globes and People's Choice Awards you keep in the bathroom\"</li><li class=\"d1\">theatrical genius of the early 3rd century BC</li><li class=\"d1\">play <i>Thais</i>, a <b>comedy</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul quotes not the Hebrew Scriptures but a <b>famous Corinthian cultural figure</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"if your priest quotes a movie in a sermon, you can't condemn them - <b>St. Paul did it first</b>\", from a comedy at that</li></ul>"},{"t":1935.55,"lv":3,"h":"Point of the quote","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Context: \"no resurrection, this life is it, <b>eat, drink and be merry</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"like <b>Frank Sinatra</b> said, live every day like you're gonna die tomorrow\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul: even <b>your own pagan dramatist</b> says bad company corrupts good morals</li><li class=\"d1\">the company you keep is a danger</li><li class=\"d0\">Same point all through the epistle</li><li class=\"d1\">go back to the old way → <b>slavery to sin, to demonic powers</b>, the same cycle of <b>self-destruction</b> Christ freed you from</li></ul>"},{"t":2006.08,"lv":2,"h":"Doctrine and life woven together","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul draws a <b>direct line</b> from content of the gospel to <b>how we live</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Christ's resurrection → proves <b>our resurrection</b> → practical effect on life</li><li class=\"d0\">Freedom or slavery, destruction or life, sin or blessing <b>directly related</b></li><li class=\"d0\">The Corinthians' <b>freedom, growth in Christ, new life</b> is only real because <b>Christ rose</b></li><li class=\"d1\">if Christ rose, we'll rise; if so, <b>we can't live that way anymore</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"You can't <b>live like a pagan and worship Christ</b>\" - his main theme through 1 Corinthians</li><li class=\"d0\">You're part of the <b>body of Christ</b>, in the community that belongs to Christ</li><li class=\"d0\">The spiritual experience you have now is the <b>proof Christ is risen</b></li><li class=\"d1\">therefore proof you will rise and <b>give an account</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2110.48,"lv":2,"h":"1 Cor 15:34 - Awake to righteousness","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>V. 34</b>: \"<b>Awake to righteousness</b> and do not sin, for some do not have the knowledge of God. I speak this to your <b>shame</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Wake up\" to righteousness = same word as <b>justice</b>, wake up to what is right, to <b>the way the world is</b></li><li class=\"d1\">set things in order now</li><li class=\"d0\">Whoever truly doesn't believe in the resurrection <b>lives like there's none</b></li><li class=\"d1\">has <b>no knowledge of God</b>, whatever knowledge they claim</li><li class=\"d0\">Throughout 1 Cor Paul condemned those with supposed <b>knowledge and wisdom</b>, <b>puffed up</b> with pride</li><li class=\"d1\">signs the knowledge of God isn't there</li><li class=\"d0\">\"I say this to your shame\"</li><li class=\"d1\">he boasts of their <b>freedom in Christ</b> and salvation as former pagans</li><li class=\"d1\">ashamed that some prove by teaching, deeds, life they <b>lack the knowledge of God</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2197.2,"lv":3,"h":"Wrap-up","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Didn't get through a big section</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>1 Corinthians 15</b> = climax of the whole epistle, <b>very dense</b> theologically</li><li class=\"d0\">Good place to end - ending the recording, then taking a few <b>live questions</b></li></ul>"}]});
